<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marmara Ereğlisi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Marmara Eregli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marmara Ereğlisi is a town on the northern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> in Tekirdağ Province, European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. It occupies the site of ancient Perinthus, a Samian colony that resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> in 340 BCE and later, renamed Heraclea, became the capital of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. Remains of the ancient city survive around the modern town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marmara-ereglisi.jpg" alt="Marmara Ereğlisi" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CeeGee · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MarmaraEre%C4%9Flisi_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Marmara Ereğlisi is a municipality and district of Tekirdağ Province with an area of 175 square kilometres. It lies 30 km east of the town of Tekirdağ and 90 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, near a small pointed headland on the north shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a>. The name distinguishes it from two other large towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> called Ereğli, a name that derives from the Greek Heraclea: Konya Ereğlisi in Konya Province and Karadeniz Ereğli on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was founded as Perinthos, usually called Perinthus in English, as a Samian colony. In about 300 CE it was renamed Heraclea. It was built like an amphitheatre on the hillside of a cape extending into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a>, close to where the modern town stands. Its port and its position at the junction of several sea routes made it a town of commercial importance, and it became known for its resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> in 340 BCE. Many of its coins survive and identify the festivals held there.</p><p>Heraclea became a Christian bishopric at an early date, according to tradition in the Apostolic Age. As a provincial capital it was the metropolitan see for all the bishoprics of its province, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, which became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 330. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> later restored its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> and palace. The see of Constantinople soon gained superiority over Heraclea, which nonetheless had five suffragan sees in the list of Pseudo-Epiphanius, fifteen in an early tenth-century list and seventeen in one of 1022 to 1025. With the Ottoman conquests the number fell sharply. Today it survives as a titular see of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecumenical-patriarch-of-constantinople">Ecumenical Patriarchate</a> and, for the Catholic Church, as Heraclea in Europa.</p><p>In 1815 Edward Daniel Clarke visited the area looking for antiquities. He found scarcely any at the village of Eski Ereğli, now Gümüşyaka in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selymbria">Silivri</a> district, and was told that the coastal village known as Büyük Ereğli, some six miles away, corresponded to ancient Heraclea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The district had a population of 29,549 in 2022. The town is a mixture of modern blocks and old country houses, with a small harbour, and the coast on either side is lined with holiday compounds used by people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. The area has two natural harbours and three small ports, with tanker ports of Botaş and Total, an LNG storage facility and a gas-fired power plant at Sultanköy. A large fault follows the coast.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marmara Ereğlisi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Rome made Thrace a province in 46 CE, it became the provincial capital, later renamed Heraclea; it is modern <b>Marmara Ereğlisi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/perinthus">Perinthus</a></li></ul></section>
